# Exercise: Real Echo Server
#
# This is the capstone. By the end of this exercise you have written
# a working TCP echo server and exercised it against a working TCP
# client — both ends running cooperatively in the same program.
#
# This is a **one-shot** echo server: it accepts one connection,
# echoes it, and returns. A multi-connection echo server would loop
# on accept instead. The test is built around one round-trip because
# the test asserts exactly one thing — that the echo round-tripped.
#
# Socket ownership (the design choice you might second-guess):
#
#   - Main owns the listener. It calls listen and closes it after
#     the assertion.
#   - The spawned strand owns *only* the accepted client connection.
#     It gets a copy of the listener via copy-on-spawn, but treats
#     it as a read-only handle for `accept` and drops the copy when
#     done — main will close the real kernel handle.
#   - Each side closes its own end of the actual TCP connection
#     (server closes the accepted client; main closes the connect
#     socket). The kernel handles both halves of a close cleanly.
#
# Strand cooperation: every `net.tcp.*` parking point gives the
# scheduler an opportunity to run the other strand. The data
# dependencies enforce the ordering — accept can't return until
# main connects; main's read can't return until echo-server's
# write completes.
#
# Your task: complete the test-echo body. The echo-server word
# is already written for you (study it; it's the canonical shape
# you'll write again). The piece you fill in is the client side
# in main: connect, write, read, assert, close.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

# seq:allow(unchecked-tcp-write)
: echo-server ( Socket -- )
    dup net.tcp.accept
    [ # ( server client )
      dup net.tcp.read
      [ # ( server client data )
        over net.tcp.write drop
      ]
      [ drop ]
      if
      net.tcp.close drop      # close the accepted client (we own this)
      drop                     # drop our stale copy of the listener
    ]
    [ # accept failed
      drop drop
    ]
    if
;

# seq:allow(unchecked-tcp-write)
: test-echo ( -- )
    18265 net.tcp.listen
    [ # ( server )
      [ echo-server ] strand.spawn drop

      # Your code here: open the client side of the echo.
      # Stack when you start: ( server )
      # Stack when you finish: ( server )  ← so the close below fires
      #
      # Steps:
      #   1. net.tcp.connect to "127.0.0.1" 18265
      #   2. on success: write "ping", read, assert response is "ping",
      #      close the client socket. Use a `[ ok ] [ err ] if` on
      #      the read Bool.
      #   3. on connect failure: drop the junk socket and fail the test.
      false test.assert                # placeholder

      # Do not edit below this line
      net.tcp.close drop               # main closes the listener
    ]
    [ drop ]
    if
;
